{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-15323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,2]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-15323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ on $[2,4]$ is given by" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2{x}^{2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-5984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/431975da27fb1ff06baa520ef4f870cb34c879f384bfc5ddd774422f15fe7b57.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=4{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}$ on $[0,2],$ as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4},$ $a=0,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4(\\frac{-2x}{4}){e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}=-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{(-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)$ on $[1,3]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote, that we use $|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$, which is valid due to that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x>0$ on $[1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-15397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-15397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x$ on $[2,6]$ is given by" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x,$ $a=2,$ and $b=6.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-x+1=1-x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=1-x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{1+(1-x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-5999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{1+(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [2,5].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}={x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{3}+(5)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}+(2)] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-346846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "42"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(2-{x}^{2})^{-1/2}(-2x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})]-[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-362546" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362546" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ $a=-1,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1-2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+x}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+1}-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-362589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-362589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{6x}-2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{6x}+2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3(1)}-{e}^{-3(1)}}{6}]-[\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}-{e}^{-3(0)}}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-352925" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e^3-e^{-3}}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{1/2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{1/2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}{(1+x)}^{3/2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[{4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-5997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{12}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{14}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{34}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-5997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{14}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x+4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x+4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{4x+4}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+1}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{x+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\sqrt{x+1}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-82622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+1}}+1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+4"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{x+1}+1"] ["math" "y=x+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\sqrt{x+1}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3\\sqrt{2-x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "y=2\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}-x+1"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{2-x}+\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-6003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{x}^{2}-4x+1=(2x-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm (2x-1).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int (2x-1)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm ({x}^{2}-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y={x}^{2}-x+4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-82624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2x-1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+x"] ["math" "y=x-2"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}-4"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}-x+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82624" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={x}^{2}-x+4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-82612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2+{sin}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "y=4+{cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "y=5-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 2{sec}^{2}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 2\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4b183965c62a7c75bfaa462ee8efd70fbadef48beebfed1b114ec3ed6029cf"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)"] ["math" "y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(4x)-1"] ["math" "y=2{tan}^{2}⁡(2x)-1"] ["math" "y=4{sec}^{2}⁡(2x)+1"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "q-82615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/state.json" "file_sha256" "8bc5e76e393d54a79c2922c1f846ad1b9846c24bb6257ad22bb566ec3b200c98"} {"question" "e-7268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-7268.json" "file_sha256" "034b1652fbb8204927408d88692e8ee063626c2ce206b060635918fd80a078df"} {"question" "e-7268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= \\arccos{x} ,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-7268.json" "file_sha256" "034b1652fbb8204927408d88692e8ee063626c2ce206b060635918fd80a078df"} {"question" "e-560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-560.json" "file_sha256" "77b631252b6483555a202b99c2e74774b9acc0947d26e35e05d031f3c2398163"} {"question" "e-560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-560.json" "file_sha256" "77b631252b6483555a202b99c2e74774b9acc0947d26e35e05d031f3c2398163"} {"question" "e-561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-561.json" "file_sha256" "946a15820022627309512b7650643ac5eab28a02f221c38e68cd24904da31930"} {"question" "e-561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/example-561.json" "file_sha256" "946a15820022627309512b7650643ac5eab28a02f221c38e68cd24904da31930"}] "usage" {"q-15397" [] "e-560" [] "e-561" [] "e-7268" [] "q-82624" []} "basis" 1146 "retractions" [[17592186083454 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\ln (\\csc x)$ on $[1, 3]$?"] [17592186083851 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y = f(x)$ is given by $∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2} - 4x}dx$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?"] [17592186083015 :question/problem "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]$?"] [17592186083015 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\arccos{x}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\ninto the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{1 - x^{2}}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1 - x^{2} + 1}{1 - x^{2}}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x^{2}}{1 - x^{2}}}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082925 :question/problem "Compute the arc length of the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/760/Source/Images/d295aff6e58a4512c5e355673a1589a0.png)"] [17592186082925 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^{3/2}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1 + [\\frac{3}{2}x^{1/2}]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4 + 9x}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4 + 9x)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n$$\n\nto the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu= 4+9x\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}∫_{4}^{16}u^{\\frac{1}{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}[\\frac{2}{3}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}] - [(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([64] - [8]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{56}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082926 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle \\int_{5}^{6}} \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}} \\,\\textrm{d}x$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?\n\n1. $y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n2. $y= \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n3. $y= \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$"] [17592186082926 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6} \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ± ∫\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 16}}dx \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 16} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is\n\n$$\ny= \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186080055 17592186080056 17592186080057 17592186080058 17592186080059 17592186082473 17592186082474 17592186082475 17592186082476 17592186082477] "immutable_fields" [[17592186080054 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080054 :answer-field/id] [17592186080054 :answer-field/key] [17592186080054 :answer-field/type] [17592186082472 :answer-field/correct] [17592186082472 :answer-field/id] [17592186082472 :answer-field/key] [17592186082472 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
